+### [Vanilla RS-NeRF](16883-vanilla-rs-nerf.md)
+A framework that reconstructs a NeRF from rolling shutter (RS) images by explicitly modeling the camera's motion trajectory during exposure and jointly optimizing NeRF parameters and camera extrinsics for each image row.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rays} \cdot n \cdot N_{pts})$, where $N_{iters}$ is training iterations, $N_{rays}$ is number of rays per batch, $n$ is number of virtual poses (7), and $N_{pts}$ is points per ray (128). This is significantly slower than standard NeRF due to the factor $n$ [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for $T_{start}$ and $T_{end}$ for each image, in addition to NeRF parameters. The intermediate sharp images are not stored simultaneously if processed sequentially, but memory usage is higher than standard NeRF [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On synthetic datasets (Deblur-NeRF, MBA-VO), BAD-NeRF achieves superior PSNR, SSIM, and lower LPIPS compared to prior works like BARF and DeblurNeRF [S2]. It also provides more accurate camera poses (lower ATE) [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Higher than standard NeRF due to the need to render $n$ virtual sharp images per pixel per image and average them. The complexity scales linearly with the number of samples $n$ [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing gradients for $T_{start}$ and $T_{end}$ for each image, in addition to the NeRF parameters [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on synthetic datasets from Deblur-NeRF (Cozy2room, Factory, Pool, Tanabata, Trolley) and MBA-VO (ArchViz-low, ArchViz-high) [S2]. Achieves superior performance compared to prior works like BARF and DeblurNeRF in terms of PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS, especially under severe motion blur [S2].
 *   **Hardware:** Tested on NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Physically grounded blur model; robust to inaccurate initial poses; recovers sharper results under severe blur [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Higher computational complexity; assumes constant velocity during exposure (linear interpolation); requires careful tuning of learning rates for poses vs. network [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Physically grounded formulation that recovers sharper results and improves pose accuracy under severe motion blur [S1]. Robust to inaccurate initialized camera poses [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Higher computational complexity compared to methods that fix poses or use compact blur approximations [S1]. Linear interpolation may not capture complex camera motions as well as splines [S2].
 
 ## Background
-BAD-NeRF was published in November 2022 [S1]. It addresses the challenge of training NeRF on motion-blurred images, which violates the standard assumption of sharp images and accurate poses [S2]. It builds on the idea of bundle adjustment, jointly optimizing scene and camera parameters. It is distinct from DeblurNeRF, which fixes poses, and BARF, which optimizes poses at a single timestamp [S2]. The method uses linear interpolation in SE(3) to model motion trajectories during exposure [S2].
+BAD-NeRF was published in November 2022 [S1]. It addresses the challenge of training NeRFs from motion-blurred images, which violate the standard assumption of sharp images and accurate camera poses [S2]. The method builds on the idea of integrating the physical image formation process of motion blur into the NeRF training pipeline [S2]. It differs from BARF and Jeong et al. by optimizing the camera motion trajectory within the exposure time rather than at a single timestamp [S2]. It also differs from DeblurNeRF by jointly estimating trajectories with NeRF parameters instead of relying on fixed poses [S2].
 
